{
  // Separate from the root tsconfig because this code has a different runtime.
  //
  // This repo has no bundler and no browser. What it has is one body of
  // TypeScript imported by three runtimes at once, which is what every choice
  // below is about.
  //
  // WHY THE CORE LIVES UNDER supabase/functions/_shared RATHER THAN catalog/src.
  // A Supabase edge function may only import files inside supabase/functions —
  // the CLI uploads that directory and nothing above it — so a discovery
  // function importing `../../../src/discover.ts` type-checks locally and fails
  // to deploy. `_shared` is Supabase's own convention for exactly this, and
  // putting the modules there means the code the function runs is the code the
  // tests run, with no copy step to forget.
  //
  // It is imported three ways and has to satisfy all of them at once:
  //
  //   * by Node, from catalog/scripts/seed.mjs, using Node 22+'s native type
  //     stripping — which requires the import specifier to carry the .ts
  //     extension, hence allowImportingTsExtensions;
  //   * by Deno, from the discovery edge function, which requires the same;
  //   * by vitest, from test/catalog/, which does not care either way.
  //
  // "moduleResolution": "nodenext" is what makes the explicit extensions legal
  // rather than an error. The root config uses "bundler", where they are not.
  //
  // Run it with: npm run typecheck
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "WebWorker"],
    "module": "nodenext",
    "moduleResolution": "nodenext",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    // WebWorker RATHER THAN DOM, and the difference is the point. This code
    // needs `fetch`, `Response`, `AbortSignal` and `setTimeout` — web-standard
    // globals that Node, Deno and the browser all have, and that the ES2022 lib
    // alone does not declare. DOM would declare them too, and would also declare
    // `document`, `window` and `localStorage`, which would let a browser-only
    // reference compile in code that runs inside an edge function and a Node
    // script. WebWorker is the platform surface these three runtimes actually
    // share.
    "types": []
  },
  // _shared ONLY, deliberately. The function entrypoints next to it are Deno
  // programs: they use `Deno.serve`, the `Deno` namespace and `jsr:` specifiers,
  // none of which tsc can resolve, and pointing it at them produces a page of
  // "Cannot find name 'Deno'" that says nothing about the code. Deno type-checks
  // those at deploy time, which is the runtime that will actually run them.
  //
  // _shared is the part that has to satisfy three runtimes at once, so it is the
  // part worth checking here.
  "include": ["supabase/functions/_shared/**/*.ts"]
}
